WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.470
Believe the hype.

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:05.810
Spider-Man Across the Spider-verse is somehow
better than the first movie.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.930
This film really just transcends every aspect
of animation and film.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.210
Like obviously the biggest draw here is the
crazily inventive and unique art style that

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:19.030
goes ham in every single frame, but Across
the Spiderverse is also a damn good movie

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.040
on its own.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:26.010
The script is filled with so much heart, emotion,
massive dramatic moments, great twists, the

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:30.010
dialogue is quick, snappy, and so down to
earth and real.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.610
And with all of this and how engaging the
plot of the movie is, it also finds time to

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:39.010
regularly make you laugh with natural jokes
that aren’t forced.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:44.239
This movie is simultaneously one of the best
dramas, action movies, and even comedies at

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:45.239
times.

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:50.060
The characters feel effortlessly funny and
the charisma exuding from everyone is so palpable.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.520
Basically, everyone here is a standout, but
Miles’ parents get a big emphasis in this

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:59.399
movie and the care they have for Miles spills
out and infects the entire movie making it

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:01.260
deeply endearing.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.560
Because of this, Miles’ drive in the later
part of the movie completely wins you over

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.650
and gives you chills from how much great setup
there is.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.650
They spoil the second-act twist in the trailer,
but I’ll refrain from saying it just in

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.380
case you don’t know about those plot details.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.880
Then all of this love is contrasted by a great
villain in Miguel O’Hara who’s played

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.030
by Oscar Isacc.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:26.049
He’s so menacing and easily steals scenes
from how big of a presence he is.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:30.840
He’s also kind of inherently designed as
the antithesis to this movie in that the movie

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.299
is really energetic, fun, and fast, but Miguel
is a depressed figure filled with sorrow,

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:38.170
anger, and an once of envy.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:42.759
The moment he shows up he stands out much
more because of how dark and serious he is.

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:47.290
He contrasts the tone and feel of the movie
and to me this makes him stand out even more

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.290
as the villain.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.600
His point of view is completely logical and
his motivation is never in question and is

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.790
also right.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:58.710
These two opposing sides reminds me of Captain
America: Civil War where each side is good,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.899
but they have fundamentally conflicting views
for how they achieve the right thing.

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:08.479
It makes the conflict that much better because
it forces Miles to make groundbreaking decisions

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:09.479
throughout the movie.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.250
He’s not just battling a generic bad guy
with flat motivations, but a character that

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.940
forces him to reevaluate who he is as a character
and superhero.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.210
Across the Spiderverse is a surprising 2 hours
and 20 minutes long which is pretty unheard

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.959
of for an animated movie.

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:30.220
This means Lord and Miller had no constraints
while making the film they wanted and it really

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.450
shows here because they were completely unleashed.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.739
Or everyone who was working on it because
the movie has multiple directors, so it was

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:40.720
probably a deeply collaborative process.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.190
Every setpiece is exploding with vibrant and
crazy energy and I think the set-piece with

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.750
all the Spidermen is probably one of my favorite
sequences in a film.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:54.670
It does not let up for a second and the attention
to detail in every shot is truly staggering.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.170
It was overwhelming but in a good way.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.110
Like no wonder why it took them so long to
make this movie.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:05.530
Also at no point does the movie feel like
it’s overly utilizing fan servicey cameos.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.049
Because there are definitely cameos within
the movie, but they never feel forced or detract

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:11.390
from what’s going on.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.700
Like there’s so much casual quick moments
that made the theater go wild and the directors

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.659
don’t dwell on it for a whole minute like
No Way Home.

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:22.860
The creators know that the story they want
to tell is the priority and don’t want to

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.530
cheap out with in-your-face fan service that
halts the momentum.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:31.659
Also speaking of momentum, the pace is lightning-fast
for 2 hours and 20 minutes.

00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:35.280
Which really isn’t a surprise due to the
pace of the first movie, but this could have

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.930
been 3-4 hours and I wouldn’t have noticed.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.720
It’s just pure bliss and the splurge in
runtime is refreshing because they can flesh

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.920
out more characters like Gwen Stacey.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.110
There’s basically an entire prologue to
the movie that tells her story and that in

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.970
of itself was amazing.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.110
It could have ended there as a short story
and I would have been happy.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:58.340
Also within this segment of the film, the
creators establish a unique visual language

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.410
in that each universe has its own distinct
style.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.830
Gwen’s world really utilized punchy and
bright colors that washed over every inch

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.000
of the frame.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.530
And they even get creative with matching things
like the background bleeding away while Gwen

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:12.530
is getting upset.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:18.070
It isn’t an understatement about how revolutionary
and creative this film is for animation and

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.699
makes the first film feel like a test run.

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:25.970
Every landscape shot is an instant screen
saver, the transitions are constantly fresh,

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.250
they play with the framerate in fun ways again
and how it impacts different characters, the

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:34.780
style of a universe carries over into the
physical character, the action is so well

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:39.000
captured, and the creators are always experimenting
visually.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.630
Unrelated to Spider-Man, there’s this scene
in the show Arcane where a character utilizes

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:49.070
time travel in a fight, and while doing so,
the art style drastically changes and turns

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:52.530
into a beautiful showcase of experimental
animation.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:58.070
Now imagine this level of creativity but spread
out over 2 hours and 20 minutes.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.320
The action is constantly putting you into
a bewildered state because of this.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.130
Across the Spiderverse is simply one of the
best and if not the best animated movie ever

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.600
made in my opinion.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:12.009
Which is actually a pretty hard title given
Pixar’s lineup.

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:16.761
Those movies may technically have better scripts,
but the presentation of Across the Spider-verse

00:05:16.761 --> 00:05:20.560
excels the movie into a league that is incomprehensible.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.310
This is already shaping up to be one of the
highest-rated movies on Letterboxd which really

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:25.780
isn’t surprising.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:30.070
If I had the option I’d probably give it
6 stars due to how much the animation blew

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.070
me away.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:35.810
I haven’t felt this level of joy and shock
from pure quality in a long time.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:41.470
Or really not that long since John Wick 4
just came out and that was an action masterpiece.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:45.720
If this is the level of creativity that animated
movies are heading towards like with how great

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:50.440
the last Puss in Boots was and hopefully the
new Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles, then this

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.919
is going to be a golden age for animation.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:56.970
Thank you for watching and for any regular
viewers I’ll put an update about the whole

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:00.190
demonetization situation in the community
tab.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.160
But let me know in the comments if this movie
exceeded your expectations because it somehow

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.749
did for me and they were sky-high.

